# React Component Patterns

## Component Structure

Use functional components with hooks. Keep components focused — one responsibility per component.

```tsx
// Good: Focused, typed component
interface UserCardProps {
  user: User;
  onSelect: (id: string) => void;
}

function UserCard({ user, onSelect }: UserCardProps) {
  return (
    <div onClick={() => onSelect(user.id)}>
      <h3>{user.name}</h3>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## useEffect Dependencies

Always specify the dependency array. Missing deps cause stale closures and subtle bugs:

```tsx
// Good: explicit deps
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUser(userId);
}, [userId]);

// Bad: missing deps, runs on every render
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUser(userId);
});
```

## State Management

Combine related state instead of multiple `useState` calls:

```tsx
// Good: grouped state
const [form, setForm] = useState({ name: '', email: '', loading: false });

// Avoid: scattered state for related values
const [name, setName] = useState('');
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
```

## Memoization

Apply memoization deliberately — only when you have a measured performance problem:

```tsx
// Memoize expensive derived values
const sortedItems = useMemo(
  () => items.slice().sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)),
  [items]
);

// Stabilize callbacks passed to memoized children
const handleSubmit = useCallback((data: FormData) => {
  onSubmit(data);
}, [onSubmit]);

// Prevent unnecessary re-renders of pure child components
const ItemList = React.memo(function ItemList({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
  return <ul>{items.map(i => <li key={i.id}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
});
```

## Custom Hooks

Extract reusable stateful logic into custom hooks:

```tsx
function useLocalStorage<T>(key: string, initial: T) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState<T>(() => {
    try {
      const stored = localStorage.getItem(key);
      return stored ? JSON.parse(stored) : initial;
    } catch {
      return initial;
    }
  });

  const set = useCallback((next: T) => {
    setValue(next);
    localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(next));
  }, [key]);

  return [value, set] as const;
}
```

## TypeScript Props

Type all props explicitly. Avoid `any`:

```tsx
// Good: explicit interface
interface ButtonProps {
  label: string;
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'ghost';
  disabled?: boolean;
  onClick: () => void;
}

// Bad: untyped
function Button({ label, onClick, ...rest }: any) { ... }
```

## Lists and Keys

Always use stable, unique keys — not array index when the list can reorder:

```tsx
// Good: stable identity
{items.map(item => <Row key={item.id} item={item} />)}

// Bad: index shifts on reorder/insert
{items.map((item, i) => <Row key={i} item={item} />)}
```

## Error Boundaries

Wrap async data sections in error boundaries to prevent full-page crashes:

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<p>Failed to load users.</p>}>
  <UserList />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

## What to Avoid

- Avoid direct DOM manipulation — use refs only when React can't do it
- Avoid storing derived values in state — compute them from existing state
- Avoid `useEffect` for data that can be derived synchronously
- Never call hooks conditionally or inside loops
